      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You raise a crucial point. While the executive privilege doctrine exists, the Supreme Court's jurisprudence has also established limitations on its application. For instance, Nixon v. Fitzgerald (1975) held that executive privilege is not always absolute, and can be overridden by Congress if evidence of executive wrongdoing clearly points to presidential involvement. Additionally, legislative stripping or withholding necessary documents can effectively implement checks on the president's power. Thus, the doctrine of executive privilege does have built-in constraints.",
      "source": "target",
